{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-49206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/assets/22472e04f7bd26076da43a2ad17a192726ad19b5bd3885ce066c9523cad4f870.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-49206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of three parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-1,1),$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle. Therefore, we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-1.5.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+3)(2) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2)(2) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-1.5+5+3 \\\\  & =6.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-49206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea52c52978867eeda2b16feac3f8ce66b2eb424c0df4713f4f31b2f85929d29c"} {"question" "q-49206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-49206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6.5"] ["math" "8.5"] ["math" "9.5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-49206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6.5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/assets/3e0eca1a04396c0c86bbf5c3e5a9b3ff7071eacfe3d8c4281549e70cda809e8c.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a right triange, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,5].$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted quarter-circle of radius $2$ and a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =5+(4-\\pi ) \\\\  & =9-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea52c52978867eeda2b16feac3f8ce66b2eb424c0df4713f4f31b2f85929d29c"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4-\\pi "] ["math" "5+\\pi "] ["math" "5-\\pi "] ["math" "9+\\pi "] ["math" "9-\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9-\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-316261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/assets/d36f2859865da58d226a738d8338f726df950db75b7421b604bb692c9049d54a.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-316261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,-2]$ and the line segment for $x\\in (-2,1].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-316261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea52c52978867eeda2b16feac3f8ce66b2eb424c0df4713f4f31b2f85929d29c"} {"question" "q-316261" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-316261" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9+\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-338507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/assets/8eb7c4fe147028c6dc31aad2427a33bb713553e610101b1fd263bda30f5c49d0.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-338507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-6,-4],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-3),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-4].$   The corresponding region is a triangle below the $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-3).$   The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0).$   The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,5].$   The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+1)(5) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{1}{2}+6+10 \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-338507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea52c52978867eeda2b16feac3f8ce66b2eb424c0df4713f4f31b2f85929d29c"} {"question" "q-338507" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-338507" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14.5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-353136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/assets/f7eebd794b43da16658750db0df3a4d4c4b894ddb80f84795ea483aa4d579f56.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-353136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in [0,4),$ and the line segment for $x\\in (4,6].$ Using the additive property of integrals, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a lower semi-circle of radius $2$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-2\\pi .\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (4,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-2\\pi +8+6 \\\\  & =14-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-353136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea52c52978867eeda2b16feac3f8ce66b2eb424c0df4713f4f31b2f85929d29c"} {"question" "q-353136" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"} {"question" "q-353136" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14-2\\pi"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "71a76076d313d08cf718478970831f58b9e49f22b60071c776ac86acc5b6f246"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1226 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
